WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.029
Agent Ellen O’Connor was used to working
normal cases in the Department of Analytics,

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:10.440
or at least, as normal as cases can be when
you work for the SCP Foundation.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.530
This usually involved analyzing data and managing
the WATCHDOG network for critical surveillance

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:16.530
information.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:21.849
SCP sites and facilities were highly guarded,
and major dangers usually stemmed from practical

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:26.269
security failures that would be dealt with
on-site by security teams.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:30.970
It was rare that she would be called down
to investigate a high-priority case in person

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.460
- but that’s exactly what happened when
she got an alert from the holding facility

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.390
containing SCP-5002.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.460
SCP-5002 was not your usual specimen.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.761
Better known as Emma Hastings, she was a seemingly
normal woman born in Tewkesbury, England where

00:00:47.761 --> 00:00:50.250
she worked as a writer of detective novels.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:56.100
Born in 1978, she had published ten books
that had found moderate success, and was living

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.340
a quiet life without much to gain the foundation’s
attention.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.239
That is, until the Foundation started observing
strange events around her.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.310
It seemed that whenever Ms. Hastings read
a copy of one of her own books, the events

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.390
would occur in real life.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.890
While some details might change, the main
narrative of her book would play out - along

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.380
with any associated deaths.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.020
The Foundation picked up on this pattern when
they intercepted UK police reports, and took

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.920
a confused Ms. Hastings into custody.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.270
She claimed to be unaware of this strange
effect, but cooperated with Foundation authorities

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:34.210
and was taken to Site-06 where she was kept
in a standard containment cell.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.040
But then came the events of December 14th,
2019.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:44.329
It was time for morning checks, and SCP-5002
was lying in bed - and she was dead.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:45.859
But what had killed her?

00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:50.659
The cell was locked with no signs of forced
entry, and Agent O’Connor quickly obtained

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:54.869
the electronic records that showed the cell
had not been disrupted.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:59.490
Security cameras confirmed no unusual activity,
and the facility’s Reality Anchors that

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.859
suppressed reality-altering powers were operational.

00:02:02.859 --> 00:02:09.289
There was no obvious explanation for SCP-5002’s
death, but something wasn’t adding up.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.790
Agent O’Connor knew she needed to investigate
everyone who could have been involved.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.569
Soon, Agent O’Connor had collected the entire
crew of Site-06.

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:19.700
Site director Evelyn May.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.260
Her Chief Medical Officer Dr. Nadine Grossenbacher.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.430
Senior researcher Dr. Karen Yau and her second-in-command
Michael Simpson.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:33.010
Security Officer Lowry, and D-Class Personnel
D-4986, who was currently stationed in Wing

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.930
G under the supervision of Officer Lowry.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:42.569
It seemed that after her death, SCP-5002 had
become the star of her very own detective

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:43.569
story.

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:45.690
The first meeting of all the suspects was
tense.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:51.689
Agent O’Connor tried to present the basics
of the case, but it soon descended into arguments.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:57.370
D-4986 had an attitude, and Officer Lowry
wasted no time in threatening the D-Class

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.370
Personnel.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.409
Junior Researcher Simpson, meanwhile, quickly
started showing contempt towards the deceased

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:06.520
Ms. Hastings, referring to her as a neutralized
specimen.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.590
Agent O’Connor let them argue among themselves,
watching and studying them, before surprising

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:18.700
them all with an announcement - One of the
people in this room was SCP-5002’s killer.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.900
Agent O’Connor had already collected much
of the material relevant to the case.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.450
She had all the police reports on the deaths
linked to works by SCP-5002, including the

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:32.950
2017 unsolved murder of British grad student
Kate Holloway.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.909
She had investigated the containment cell
that SCP-5002 had been held in, including

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.599
all the normal amenities and the unique ones
she had been given - like her electronic typewriter,

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:46.270
a bottle of whiskey, and a fully-typed manuscript.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:50.650
It seemed that SCP-5002 hadn’t let her containment
stop her writing.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:56.110
Now, it was time to start interviewing the
suspects - starting with Officer Lowry.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.670
Officer Lowry started out friendly, but seemed
to tense when it came discussing the morning

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:01.930
he discovered SCP-5002’s body.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:08.970
He had been escorting D-4986, who had been
kept in a spare containment chamber, to his

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.550
standard duties and checked briefly on the
specimens along the way.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.430
But when he jiggled the handle of the cell
and no response came from Ms. Hasting’s

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.019
chamber, he investigated.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.870
He could see that there was blood all over
the blanket, and Officer Lowry, now accompanied

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.069
by Dr. Yau, investigated the cell to determine
what had happened.

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:32.229
Chief Medical Officer Nadine Grossenbacher,
recently promoted to her position, was next

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:33.630
to be interrogated.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:39.160
She reported that SCP-5002 had been stabbed
at least ten times by a sharp object, but

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.080
there were no signs of a struggle and no murder
weapon was found in the cell.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.990
While Dr. Grossenbacher was helpful in explaining
the cause of death and in pointing out that

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:53.850
the site director seemed to have a special
interest in SCP-5002, she was cagey when asked

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:59.090
about her own whereabouts during the hours
of 1 and 3 AM - the likely time period during

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.380
which SCP-5002 was killed.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.150
The picture was not getting any clearer for
Agent O’Connor.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.440
Officer Lowry was brought back in for more
questioning about the facility’s security

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.440
systems.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.710
It seemed like his shift was well-covered
- except for his smoke break that he took

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:18.600
around 2 AM, where he left every night and
left the low-risk containment area unwatched.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.069
He had only been gone fifteen minutes, and
swore that any security breaches were unlikely

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:26.490
due to the need for high-level clearance.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:32.169
Lowry continued to cooperate, but was quickly
becoming tense at being seen as a suspect.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:34.720
However, not everyone was as cooperative.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.819
A security alert soon went up - one of the
suspects had tried to leave the facility.

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:44.810
It was quickly identified as Junior Researcher
Michael Simpson, and his place in the interrogation

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.020
list moved up.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.539
He was belligerent and refused to give information
about why he needed to leave, and claimed

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:55.050
to have an alibi - he was working on his thesis
during the time of the murder.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:00.220
He confirmed Officer Lowry’s story of trying
to rouse SCP-5002 in the morning, but claimed

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:04.830
to know nothing about a kitchen knife found
in the staff kitchenette dishwasher - conveniently

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:08.060
washed clean of any identifying substances.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:13.290
Agent O’Connor may have found the murder
weapon, but the killer had covered their tracks.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:19.789
Junior Researcher Simpson made one thing very
clear - he did not like SCP-5002 and was not

00:06:19.789 --> 00:06:21.750
particularly sorry that she was dead.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:28.190
He viewed all SCPs the same - as inhuman specimens
unworthy of any respect.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.979
He had worked with her extensively in testing,
but that didn’t seem to change his mind.

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.800
But he did have kind words for his supervisor,
Dr. Yau - who had given him a copy of her

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.349
key card, giving him greater access to the
facility.

00:06:41.349 --> 00:06:46.629
Agent O’Connor decided that she would need
to look a little deeper into SCP-5002’s

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:48.330
history at the facility.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:55.290
A security video of a previous test of SCP-5002’s
abilities provided some new information, and

00:06:55.290 --> 00:07:01.690
it just so happened to involve Dr. Yau, Michael
Simpson, Officer Lowry - and a new face, a

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:05.220
female D-Class personnel with the classification
of D-2825.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:13.099
The scientists would test SCP-5002’s powers
by having her write things down about D-2825

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:18.240
and then read them aloud - and then watch
as D-2825 would act them out.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.270
They would primarily test this by using a
machine with two buttons on it, one red and

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.430
one green.

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:30.330
5002 would write out and then read a passage
describing D-2825 pressing a sequence of red

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.659
and green buttons to see if she would follow
it.

00:07:33.659 --> 00:07:40.110
2825 followed the sequence exactly as SCP-5002
had written it, but only once.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:44.110
If SCP-5002 read it again, there was no effect.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:49.410
While it worked when some of the text was
redacted, it seemed to make D-2825 deeply

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.400
upset and confused - as if she was being puppeted.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:08:01.159
Both SCP-5002 and D-2825 were led back to
their cells, but not before SCP-5002 asked

00:08:01.159 --> 00:08:07.610
a disturbing question - What if D-2825 had
been restrained when the text was read?

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.069
The investigation then shifted to Dr. Yau,
who had worked more closely with SCP-5002

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:14.159
than anyone.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:19.349
Agent O’Connor asked about the book SCP-5002
had written while in custody, and was told

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:22.340
that it was about the life of a D-class personnel.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:27.259
Dr. Yau said that it had been very different
from her normal works, and ruled out one of

00:08:27.259 --> 00:08:31.139
Agent O’Connor’s theories - there had
been nothing in the book resembling the events

00:08:31.139 --> 00:08:33.700
of SCP-5002’s death.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.870
She had not found a way to self neutralize.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:40.130
But Dr. Yau did reveal a few important details.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:45.610
The whiskey found in SCP-5002’s cell had
not been authorized by her, and Michael Simpson

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:51.360
had begun acting cold and hostile towards
SCP-5002 several weeks into testing.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.390
She praised Dr. Grossenbacher though, and
her impressive work with D-2825 even after

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.040
the D-Class suffered a mental breakdown.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.310
But there was one suspect that stood out in
Dr. Yau’s mind - and would soon become Agent

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:06.220
O’Connor’s next target.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:11.570
D-4986 had been hostile since the investigation
began, more so than the average D-class.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:18.130
Dr. Yau reported that he had smiled when he
saw that SCP-5002 was dead and expressed that

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.110
she got what she had coming.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:25.390
Agent O’Connor knew she needed to get the
D-class into an interrogation room but first

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.680
she needed more information on him - starting
with finding out why he was in Foundation

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:31.839
custody.

00:09:31.839 --> 00:09:37.240
The incident report wasn’t pretty - seven
murder convictions, with three others suspected.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:42.040
And all ten deaths were by the same method
- brutal stabbings.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:47.790
D-4986 immediately admitted that he was guilty
of those murders and had no regrets, saying

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.430
all his victims got what they had coming.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:55.140
But he claimed that he did not kill SCP-5002
- despite wanting her dead.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:01.280
He had long hated SCP-5002 and Dr. Yau for
using him as a puppet, forcing him to perform

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.120
tasks against his will.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:09.510
While D-2825 had broken down, D-4986 was filled
with rage.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:15.960
Was D-4986 truly innocent - or had he been
used against his will one more time?

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.580
Upon examining the residents of the site,
Agent O’Connor decided to investigate another

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.250
potential suspect - a fellow SCP.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.779
Better known as Robert Gates, this United
Kingdom-born man’s SCP number has been classified,

00:10:29.779 --> 00:10:34.920
but we know he possessed the ability to pass
through solid objects and was in Foundation

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.510
custody after using his ability to assault
more than thirty women.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:43.940
Believed to be a dangerous criminal with antisocial
personality disorder, he was kept under the

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:49.860
influence of a reality anchor that neutralized
his powers - but had he found a way to break

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.860
free?

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.820
Mr. Gates seemed to know more about the death
of SCP-5002 than he should, and wasted no

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.769
time taunting Agent O’Connor.

00:10:57.769 --> 00:11:02.320
He claimed to be able to leave whenever he
wanted, despite past testing showing he was

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.990
safely confined.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:08.420
He claimed to be innocent of the murder and
said he never interacted with or assaulted

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:14.070
SCP-5002, but he worked very hard to put doubt
in Agent O’Connor’s mind.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:19.320
He wanted them to know that it was only a
matter of time before he found his way out.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:23.750
There was only one person left who might be
able to provide some insight about the whole

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:24.970
affair.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:29.810
Director Evelyn May was a busy woman, and
she had waited until last to be interviewed.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:35.100
She seemed preoccupied with other disasters,
worried about the paperwork, but cooperated

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:39.829
when Agent O’Connor wanted to review the
data on the reality anchors for the site.

00:11:39.829 --> 00:11:43.290
As the interview went on though, things became
tense.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:47.110
Agent O’Connor mentioned that Simpson had
been under pressure to produce results with

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.720
SCP-5002, and Director May bristled at the
implications.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.790
Agent O’Connor was able to get information
out of Director May about her whereabouts

00:11:55.790 --> 00:12:00.650
at the time of the murder though - she had
been offsite meeting with a divorce attorney.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:06.579
There was something odd about that day though,
for some reason she only learned SCP-5002

00:12:06.579 --> 00:12:10.180
had been killed almost an hour later than
her body was found.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:13.760
Why had Dr. Yau kept the events from her boss
for so long?

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.019
Agent O’Connor believed she was getting
close to a conclusion, but she needed to dig

00:12:18.019 --> 00:12:20.040
deeper into Dr. Yau.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:26.070
The senior researcher seemed to have the closest
bond to SCP-5002, and a look at a past interview

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:27.160
confirmed this.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:33.760
They spent time talking about SCP-5002’s
new novel, and SCP-5002 revealed that she

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:37.910
had some previous awareness of her abilities
before being apprehended.

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:43.620
They discussed her plans for the plot, and
SCP-5002 expressed overall satisfaction with

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:45.010
her living quarters.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.010
Agent O’Connor now had all the evidence
she needed.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.990
It was time to gather the suspects.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:55.260
After Agent O’Connor brought all the players
together, she revealed that she knew they

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.649
all had been lying to her…

00:12:57.649 --> 00:13:01.019
Everyone except for D-4986.

00:13:01.019 --> 00:13:04.519
She had pinpointed weak spots in each of their
stories.

00:13:04.519 --> 00:13:09.279
Officer Lowry had lied about his smoke break,
having been gone longer than he said - and

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:11.040
drinking in addition to smoking.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.410
When he realized his drinking on duty would
be discovered, he had stashed the bottle in

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:20.910
the one place he first had the opportunity
- SCP-5002’s cell.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.649
Director May, meanwhile, had lied about her
motivations.

00:13:23.649 --> 00:13:25.519
She was being blackmailed.

00:13:25.519 --> 00:13:30.040
She had been covering up the consistent failure
of reality anchors around the facility, which

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:32.810
had allowed a dangerous SCP to get loose.

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:41.110
But Robert Gates’ target had not been SCP-5002
- it had been the unfortunate D-2825, who

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.240
experienced a breakdown as a result.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:47.649
And the source of the blackmail had been one
of the few other people with access to the

00:13:47.649 --> 00:13:53.690
statistics - Dr. Grossenbacher, whose quick
promotion suddenly made a lot more sense.

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:58.800
Dr. Grossenbacher didn’t even know the full
story - but Agent O’Connor did.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.009
The reality anchors weren’t randomly malfunctioning
- they were targeted.

00:14:03.009 --> 00:14:06.940
None of the other cells ever malfunctioned,
only Mr. Gates’.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:12.170
He was placed in close quarters with SCP-5002
with the intention of him targeting her by

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:17.040
Director May - or as she used to be known,
per a conversation with her divorce lawyer

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.670
in London, Eve Holloway.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:24.870
The mother of the late Kate Holloway, one
of the people who died because of SCP-5002’s

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:26.220
anomaly.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:30.380
She had been plotting for a long time to make
her suffer.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.480
But she wasn’t the one who brought the knife
into the facility.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:38.350
Agent O’Connor then turned to Junior Researcher
Simpson, who had his own agenda.

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:42.420
His stony and cold facade was all a cover
for his participation in the radical group

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:46.230
known as The Serpent’s Hand, which attempted
to liberate SCPs.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:52.279
He had planned to break into SCP-5002’s
cell and liberate her that night, giving her

00:14:52.279 --> 00:14:55.339
the kitchen knife for protection and escape
with her.

00:14:55.339 --> 00:15:00.329
Simpson was shocked when SCP-5002 refused
to leave - because she had something keeping

00:15:00.329 --> 00:15:01.350
her there.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:06.660
She was in love with Dr. Yau, and they were
in a relationship - and that was the primary

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.410
reason she was so content in her captivity.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:16.209
Dr. Yau was in love with SCP-5002, or Emma
as she called her, and had been intentionally

00:15:16.209 --> 00:15:21.769
slowing down her research to ensure she stayed
in her facility for as long as possible.

00:15:21.769 --> 00:15:26.000
She had been editing the footage to keep their
trysts a secret - and it would have worked

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.529
if Agent O’Connor hadn’t noticed the timestamps
while investigating the mysterious case of

00:15:30.529 --> 00:15:32.390
the Jack Daniels bottle.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:37.269
It seemed there were many people with ulterior
motives - but none of them with a reason to

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:40.220
kill SCP-5002.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:46.140
The pattern of the blood indicated that SCP-5002
had been stabbed standing up, not in her sleep,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.259
and then dragged back to her bed.

00:15:48.259 --> 00:15:50.100
It wasn’t an ambush murder.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:52.149
There was likely a confrontation.

00:15:52.149 --> 00:15:57.209
And after eliminating all the other suspects,
Agent O’Connor had pieced together the timeline

00:15:57.209 --> 00:15:58.399
of the events.

00:15:58.399 --> 00:16:04.009
Junior Researcher Simpson had made his attempt
to get SCP-5002 out of her cell, but she had

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:05.519
rejected the escape attempt.

00:16:05.519 --> 00:16:09.790
In his hurry to avoid detection, he had left
the knife behind in the cell and left the

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:11.110
cell door unlocked.

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:16.500
Just in time for Officer Lowry to jiggle the
handle as he walked by and find it unlocked.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:20.699
He was drunk and he picked a fight with SCP-5002.

00:16:20.699 --> 00:16:24.139
In the argument, the truth came out about
her and Dr. Yau.

00:16:24.139 --> 00:16:28.720
Officer Lowry felt she was flaunting it, taunting
him, and he couldn’t handle it.

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:32.480
He believed Dr. Yau should have loved him
- not SCP-5002.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:38.440
Dr. Yau had always had a good relationship
with Lowry, and ignored the signs of trouble

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:41.560
on his profile - like his history of drinking.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:46.410
She didn’t realize he was becoming obsessed
with her, and once he lost his mind and stabbed

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:52.810
SCP-5002, he did his best to cover it up - and
he would have gotten away with it, if it wasn’t

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:55.480
for the mystery of the Jack Daniels bottle.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:17:00.399
Officer Lowry was arrested for murder, but
Agent O’Connor still had questions.

00:17:00.399 --> 00:17:04.250
The final report on the case would indicate
her great doubts about the outcome.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:08.430
When dealing with a reality-warper, is anything
as clear-cut as it appears?

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:13.860
While the reality anchors in SCP-5002’s
cell were active and there were no texts detailing

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:18.310
the events of her murder, Agent O’Connor
wondered if her ability could have triggered

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:19.310
it.

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:23.240
She examined several blank pages found in
the containment cell that had been cut into

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:24.810
an elaborate pattern.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.900
She wondered if when they were placed on the
right pages, if they could be some kind of

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:31.690
grille cipher, revealing a new meaning.

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:35.870
While she didn’t have any answers, Agent
O’Connor believed that the ambiguity of

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:39.730
the case possibly warranted leniency for Officer
Lowry.

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:46.560
But she was still left with one big question
- in a world with specimens like SCP-5002,

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:52.680
who can rewrite reality, how much of anyone’s
story is truly their own?

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:57.620
For more on the complex nature of the stories
of our lives - and how they can be influenced

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:04.990
- check out “SCP-4001 - Alexandria Eternal”
or watch “SCP-343 - God” for what might

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:08.280
be the most powerful reality-warper in the
Foundation’s custody.

